/// lifetime: the heap probe that the driver lifetime tests share.
//
// A run of the driver owns its session and its analysis. It releases both on
// every path out. No other test sees a release that is missing: the
// gate sanitizers instrument the C compiler and not the x86-64 program that
// the harness runs.
//
// The heap sees one. Many runs of one command line ask for the same blocks
// in the same order. A run that gives the blocks back leaves the heap where
// it was. A run that keeps one block adds the bytes of that block to the heap
// in each round.
//
// The probe holds the live view of support/heap.ft and no other view. The
// live view counts the bytes of each block that the allocator does not hold as
// free, and the bytes of each mmap block. A kept block is in that count,
// whatever its size and wherever the allocator put it. So the view moves by
// the kept bytes of a round times the rounds between the two samples.
//
// With nothing kept, the view rises in the first rounds and then stops. The
// rise is above BOUND, so the early sample must come after the last move.
// At b6676ced, e0080544 and bda63b82 the checked mode under glibc 2.43 rose
// by 4928 bytes in 29 moves, and the last move came at round 698. Its early
// sample comes at round 2000, 1302 rounds later. Under glibc 2.39 the emitter
// mode rose by up to 27312 bytes, and the last move came by round 10. Its
// early sample comes at round 400. Each other mode stopped by round 266. A
// change that makes the rise go on past the early sample fails a probe with
// nothing kept. The cause of the rise is read in glibc, not measured: the
// view counts the free blocks in the thread cache of glibc, and the first
// rounds fill that cache.
//
// The probe does not hold the program break (the system view) or the address
// of a fresh block (the block view). The break moves when a change moves only
// the order or the size of the allocations.
// Under glibc 2.43 the program break of the emitter mode changed between two
// values 1896448 bytes apart from one round to the next, with nothing kept.
// glibc gave the top of the heap back to the system after some rounds and
// kept it after others. A tree in which gen.gen_main released four argument
// lists at its exit put the early round in one state and the 16 late rounds
// in the other, and the emitter probe failed with a live distance of 0. A
// tree that added one 8-byte field to types.layout did the same. The block
// view moves with nothing kept: in the checked mode under glibc 2.43 it read
// 400 bytes at each of the three trees.
// Each release that those two views saw, the live view sees (below).
//
// The view is necessary and not sufficient. A round must also reach the
// allocations of the release that it claims to witness. A deletion of a
// release that a round never reaches moves nothing. Each test file gives the
// program of its round, and names the releases that it is the witness of.
//
// Each mode of the driver is a test file of its own, and this module is what
// those files share. One mode in each program keeps every program below the
// 60 s that the harness gives a program. One mode in each
// program also prevents an earlier mode from fragmenting its heap. The loop
// is the first action because the probe measures allocator behavior.
//
// At b6676ced, 57 releases on the paths of the six modes were deleted one at
// a time: each deletion was compiled into the six probes, and each probe ran
// under glibc 2.39 and 2.43. The compiler refused one deletion
// (`del(s)` in check.check_free). 49 deletions made at least one probe fail,
// and the live view moved by 24032 bytes or more in each failing probe. These
// are the same probes that the largest of the system, block and live
// distances failed. 7 deletions moved no probe. 5 of them delete a release
// that a second call makes again: the direct call of `analysis_release` in
// driver.compile_entry, which its deferred call covers, and `index.free(&ix)`
// and the three releases in index.free, which the deferred block of
// driver.check_document covers. 2 have no witness: `syms_free(&g->declared)`
// in gen.gen_free and `del(ck->sized)` in check.check_free are the only
// releases of their blocks, but no round fills those blocks. The emitter
// fills `declared` only for the bodiless functions of an interface module,
// and the checker fills `sized` only for an array whose layout is pending.
//
// Then the releases of the session, the analysis, the emitter, the index and
// the pools moved into the drops of their types: gen.drop, check.drop,
// session.drop, diag.drop, diag.sources_drop, ast.arena_drop, index.drop and
// containers.pool_drop. gen.gen_free, check.check_free, index.free and the
// other frees by pointer of these types move their value into their drop.
// session.free and driver.analysis_free free each field through the free of
// its type. driver.check_document and driver.ast_entry hold their index and
// their arena in an allocation, so no second call makes a release again. On
// that tree 48 releases were deleted one at a time from the drops and the
// driver, a 49th mutant made the loop of pool_drop an `if` that frees one
// block of the chain, and the six probes ran under glibc 2.39. 45 of the 49
// made at least one probe fail, the `if` the emitter and the index probe. The
// compiler refused 2: a deleted `del(s)` of check.drop and `del(n)` of
// ast.arena_drop leave an own local that the next round of the loop
// overwrites. 2 moved no probe, for the reason above: `del(g.declared.items)`
// of gen.drop and `del(ck.sized)` of check.drop. The compiler refuses each of
// these two, because the drop then leaves an owner that the leak rule does not
// prove (D17.20). The drop of an own pointer local, as
// `session.drop_made`, cannot go: part 1 of the leak rule refuses the program
// without it.
//
// Then each end that freed a value and then took a `del` of an owner that the
// free releases, or that called a free in a loop on a chain, ended through the
// drop of its type: the scopes of check_stmt, the functions and the modules of
// the lowering, the dataflows, the place memos, the context of a proof, the FIR
// parser, the pools of the driver and the arenas of modules. On c04d6836, 78
// releases of the frees and drops in src/fort, src/lsp and std that this
// touched were deleted one at a time, and 44 turned a test red. With the drop
// form, 103 were deleted on macOS and each turned a test red, so no deletion
// that turned a test red before passes after. Of the 76 in src/fort, the
// compiler refused 12 and the aggregate report saw 57. The six probes failed
// for 6 of the other 7: the statements of each block of fir.func_drop, each
// function of fir.module_drop, the two sets of each block of
// fir_flow.drop_sets, the list of each node of ast.arena_drop and the bytes of
// each pool block. The set of each block of owner.alias.drop reaches no probe,
// and owner/prover_test fails for it.
//
// This module uses no function-pointer dispatch table. A repeated analysis
// must not grow the heap.

import capture;
import fort.driver;
import heap;
import lower_env;
import std.io;
import std.libc;
import std.os;
import std.strbuf;
import std.sys;

// The rounds of a front-end mode, and the distance in bytes that fails it.
//
// The live view of each mode rises in its first rounds with nothing kept (see
// above). The early sample comes at half the rounds, round 2000 here, 1302
// rounds after the last such move that was measured. The rounds then make a
// small leak large: a leak of 32 bytes, the smallest block of glibc, moves the
// view by 64000 bytes over the 2000 rounds between the samples, more than 15
// times BOUND.
u64 ROUNDS = 4000;
u64 BOUND = 4096;

// How many of the last rounds the probe holds against the early round. A leak
// moves the live view in every late round, so the smallest distance costs
// nothing. It keeps a late round in which the thread cache of glibc holds one
// block more or less than at the early round from failing the probe.
u64 LATE_SAMPLES = 16;

// The exit status that a refused program leaves. The mode that compiles a
// refused program asserts this status of its first round.
i32 EXIT_COMPILE_ERROR = 1;

// Measures a front-end mode over ROUNDS iterations.
fn measure(string@ args) u64 {
    return measure_rounds(args, ROUNDS);
}

// The same over a round count of its own. The emitter mode and the index mode
// compile larger programs, so fewer rounds reach the same bound.
fn measure_rounds(string@ args, u64 rounds) u64 {
    return measure_status(args, rounds, 0);
}

// The same, for a mode whose command line ends in a diagnostic: the program
// of the checked mode is refused by the checker. So its rounds exit 1 and a
// round that exits 0 is the broken one. The result is the distance of the
// live view between the early round and the nearest of the late rounds.
fn measure_status(string@ args, u64 rounds, i32 want_status) u64 {
    u64 mut early = 0;
    u64 mut least = 0;
    bool mut sampled = false;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < rounds) {
        // The driver writes to both descriptors. The test captures them, so
        // that the stdout of the test is its one line.
        i32 saved_out = capture.begin(io.STDOUT, "out.txt");
        i32 saved_err = capture.begin(io.STDERR, "err.txt");
        i32 status = driver.run(args);
        capture.end(io.STDERR, saved_err);
        capture.end(io.STDOUT, saved_out);
        if (i == 0) {
            // The first round answers for the rest. A round that stops early
            // does not make the allocations of a pass that the probe claims
            // to watch. And a probe that watches nothing is green. The
            // emitter mode ran for a whole afternoon over a program with a
            // type error: `-S` stopped at the front end. And every deletion
            // of a release in gen.gen_free was unwitnessed.
            assert(status == want_status);
        }
        if (i == rounds / 2) {
            early = heap.live_view();
        }
        if (i + LATE_SAMPLES >= rounds) {
            u64 now = heap.distance(heap.live_view(), early);
            if (!sampled || now < least) {
                least = now;
            }
            sampled = true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    assert(early != 0 && sampled);
    return least;
}

fn write_file(string path, string text) void {
    assert(io.write_file(path, cast(text, u8@)));
}

// Every closure holds `std.rt`. The driver reads it from `<std-dir>/rt.ft` and
// looks for that directory in `std` beside the program when nothing names one.
// These runs give `fort` as argv[0], so the directory is `std` in the test's
// working directory. The driver
// needs a file there that parses, and not a runtime: these tests run nothing
// that they build.
fn write_runtime() void {
    assert(os.make_dir("std", 0o700) || sys.errno() == libc.EEXIST);
    write_file("std/rt.ft", "// The empty runtime of a driver test.\n");
}

// The same with lower_env.RUNTIME as `std/rt.ft`: the runtime entry of each check kind, str_eq
// and the print functions. A mode that emits a module needs it for a program with a check or a
// print, because the emitter refuses a function whose check or print has no runtime function.
fn write_check_runtime() void {
    assert(os.make_dir("std", 0o700) || sys.errno() == libc.EEXIST);
    strbuf.str_buf mut b = strbuf.create();
    defer del(b.data);
    for (string line : lower_env.RUNTIME) {
        strbuf.append(&b, line);
        strbuf.push(&b, '\n');
    }
    write_file("std/rt.ft", strbuf.view(&b));
}
